# exempt: tenant-loop worker (workers::tenant_harness::run_multi_tenant). Its ONE literal begin_account_scoped( call, in collect_account_stats, is the consumer arm -- app.account_id, the pre-existing OPS-4 consumer-plane mechanism, permanent, and it carries an explicit account_id = $1 predicate as its primary scoping mechanism -- which is why this file is exempt and not converted. The rollup ROW it writes lands on the CONTROL pool on either plane (platform_metrics_daily/platform_active_users are control-only tables that do not exist on a tenant DB), so t.pool is only ever read from. Its account roster read (SELECT id FROM accounts) is a control table outside 0009's roster and needs no scope. CORRECTED (this PR): this entry previously held workers/reembed.rs's rationale ("consumer-only worker ... never runs against a tenant/org pool at all"), which is false of this file -- the two entries' rationales had been swapped, and both are restored. The org arm's read, collect_tenant_stats, is still AMBIENT: seven count(*) queries straight against t.pool. Six hit RLS-covered tenant tables (atoms, graphs, conversations, llm_usage, activity_events, mcp_tool_calls); the seventh reads `queries`, which 0009 §3f deliberately leaves uncovered, so only the first six are exposed. It is NOT covered by "the harness hands out a connection-scoped app.tenant_actor=system pool" -- PR #388 (21980710) deleted that STARTUP-packet option because a connection pooler rejects unknown startup parameters. Its only cover is the owner exemption (0009 §7 does not FORCE row security), asserted at pool build by tenancy::registry::worker_pool_rls_exemption_breach, so at OPS-4-T the pool refuses to build and the rollup stops with a logged error rather than reporting zeroes. collect_tenant_stats's own doc already names the fix (one short system-scoped read transaction around the seven counts) and why it is safe here; it is left to that conversion rather than done in this PR, which converts only arms whose query this file issues under a JobScope split.
