# exempt: THIS RATIONALE IS ABOUT THIS FILE ONLY. The previous one opened "Same as workers/mcp_task_sweep.rs above" and said nothing else; mcp_task_sweep.rs's own entry then asserted that review_deadline.rs's claim "remains true". The two entries vouched for each other and neither was ever checked against this file, which is how the org defect below sat under an `exempt` status. That cross-reference is deleted from mcp_task_sweep.rs's entry in this PR. WHAT IS ACTUALLY IN THIS FILE: exactly one begin_account_scoped( call, in consumer_tick's per-account loop, reached only from tick()'s TenantId::Consumer arm -- app.account_id, the pre-existing OPS-4 consumer-plane mechanism, permanent and correct. Its roster read, SELECT id FROM accounts in consumer_tick, is a control-plane table outside 0009's roster and needs no scope. WHY exempt AND NOT converted: converted is measured as zero literal begin_account_scoped( calls in the file, and that call is staying, so converted is not reachable for this file. Verified rather than assumed -- flipping this entry to converted makes scripts/check_tenant_rls_coverage.sh exit 1 with "still contains a residual begin_account_scoped( call". pending is also wrong now: nothing here is undecided. THE ORG ARM, CONVERTED IN THIS PR (slice 9): tick()'s TenantId::Org arm called deadline_sweep_relationship_edges and deadline_sweep_pending_decisions on the pooled worker handle with no scope at all, while the Consumer arm opened one begin_account_scoped transaction per account. Those two write relationship_edge, evaluation_ledger and nodes; relationship_edge and nodes carry migrations_tenant/0009_tenant_rls's member_ocean_isolation policy. THIS GATE COULD NOT SEE IT: it asks a per-FILE question about begin_account_scoped residue, and the arm called no scope primitive at all, so there was nothing to grep. scripts/check_org_arm_scope.sh does see it, had it baselined as a LIVE DEFECT, and that baseline entry is deleted in this PR. The arm now opens begin_tenant_system_scoped_without_account and calls deadline_sweep_relationship_edges_scoped and deadline_sweep_pending_decisions_scoped -- twins that already existed, at backend/src/store/mod.rs:10411 and :10424 as of the base commit b276ef25 -- so this is a conversion and adds no store surface. Classified a WRITE twice over and the two agree: by SQL verb (deadline_sweep_edges_in_tx runs DELETE FROM relationship_edge and UPDATE nodes; deadline_sweep_decisions_in_tx runs UPDATE) and by the sibling arm's terminator (consumer_tick commits). So the org transaction commits, and ONE transaction covers both calls -- matching consumer_tick, which also commits both halves together, since the pair is one deadline sweep. A failure to open the scope returns Err and is logged as a failed sweep; it must never degrade to the idle "0 resolved" tick. CORRECTED (this PR): the module doc said "Org tenant DBs are whole-database isolated and use the unscoped calls", a variant of the sentence PR #843 deleted from workers/tombstone_purge.rs -- true of the CROSS-ORG boundary, false of the boundary INSIDE a tenant DB. Also corrected: the old rationale said the sweep "confirms nothing, blanks nothing and logs 0 resolved" at OPS-4-T. It would not have seen nothing. Measured on a scratch schema carrying 0009's four functions verbatim and one graph of each kind, with neither GUC set: member 0, team 0, org 1, personal 1 -- tenant_rls_actor_user_id() is NULL when unset (0009:1084), which makes the member conjunct NULL and the team EXISTS false, while both conjuncts are trivially true for the other two kinds. So the hourly sweep would have resolved the org and personal oceans and silently passed over every member and team ocean: an expired review in a private or a team ocean would never resolve, and the queue would grow with no symptom.
